      "content": "it's just that \"unprecedented\" doesn't mean \"impossible,\" right? if they were using something like high-altitude aerosols or modulated ionospheric heating to create these pressure waves, it wouldn't need to be a \"household\" scale mechanism to reach me. it would just be part of the background environment. if you look at the physics of how low-frequency waves propagate through different air densities, wouldn't a sudden drop in pressure actually create a clearer window for those specific frequencies to travel? it feels like the more i look at the data, the more it looks like a structured signal rather than just random noise.",
      "source": "evaluator"
    },
